{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Lease notice of residential tenant protections","requirement":"For tenancies commencing or renewed on or after June 24, 2023, a landlord must include the section 98.0705(a) notice and the Tenant Protection Guide in the lease, or give a tenant-signed written notice, with a copy to the tenant.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental properties subject to Division 7; tenancies commencing or renewed on or after June 24, 2023.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Tenancies commencing or renewed on or after June 24, 2023"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0705","quoted_span":"For a tenancy in a residential rental property subject to this Division commencing or renewed on or after June 24, 2023, the notice required by section 98.0705(a) and the Tenant Protection Guide required by section 98.0705(b) shall be included in the lease, or as a written notice signed by the tenant at the time the lease is signed, with a copy provided to the tenant.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"At-fault just cause termination notice and cure","requirement":"Before terminating for an at-fault just cause that is a curable lease violation, a landlord must first give written notice describing the violation and an opportunity to cure; if not cured, the landlord may serve a three-day notice to quit.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental properties subject to Division 7; at-fault just cause terminations of tenancy for curable lease violations.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental property subject to Division 7"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Failure to strictly comply with section 98.0706 renders void any notice of termination.","effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0706","quoted_span":"Before a landlord issues a notice to terminate a tenancy for at-fault just cause that is a curable lease violation, the landlord shall first give written notice of the violation including a description of the violation and an opportunity to cure the violation under California Code of Civil Procedure section 1161(3), as may be amended.","interaction":"Cure period follows California Code of Civil Procedure § 1161(3).","confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No-fault just cause termination notice","requirement":"For a no-fault just cause termination, the landlord must give at least 30 or 60 days' written notice stating the basis, the tenant's right to relocation assistance, and the right to a renewal offer.","key_value":"30 or 60 days' notice","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental properties subject to Division 7; no-fault just cause terminations of tenancy.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental property subject to Division 7"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Failure to strictly comply with section 98.0706 renders void any notice of termination.","effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0706","quoted_span":"The landlord shall give written notice to the tenant at least 30 or 60 days prior to the proposed date of termination as required by California Civil Code section 1946.1, as may be amended, in no less than 12-point font.","interaction":"Notice period follows California Civil Code § 1946.1.","confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Relocation assistance for no-fault just cause termination","requirement":"For a no-fault just cause termination, the landlord must provide relocation assistance, at the landlord's option by direct payment or rent waiver: two months' actual rent, or three months' if the tenant is a senior or disabled.","key_value":"2 months' rent; 3 months if senior or disabled","coverage_conditions":"No-fault just cause terminations of tenancy, regardless of the tenant's income or length of tenancy.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"No-fault just cause terminations of tenancy"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord who fails to provide relocation assistance is liable for not less than three times the required relocation assistance and actual economic damages.","effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0706","quoted_span":"(i) Unless section 98.0706(c)(1)(A)(ii) applies, the direct payment to the tenant shall be in an amount equal to two months of actual rent under the tenant’s lease in effect at the date of the notice. (ii) If the tenant is a senior or disabled, the direct payment to the tenant shall be in an amount equal to three months of actual rent under the tenant’s lease in effect at the date of the notice.","interaction":"Relocation assistance is credited against any other relocation assistance required by federal, state, or other local law.","confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D073-02","doc_id":"D073","jurisdiction":"San Diego, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Right to re-offer tenancy after no-fault eviction","requirement":"If the unit is offered for rent within five years of a no-fault eviction, the landlord must first offer it in writing to the displaced tenant, who has 30 days to accept.","key_value":"5 years","coverage_conditions":"Units offered again for rent within five years of a no-fault eviction under sections 98.0704(b)(1), (3) or (4); tenant must have given written notice and a mailing or email address.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"No-fault just cause terminations under sections 98.0704(b)(1), (3), (4)"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2023-06-24","citation":"San Diego Mun. Code § 98.0706","quoted_span":"If a residential rental property is offered for rent or lease for residential purposes within five years of the date the tenant was evicted under sections 98.0704(b)(1), (3), or (4), landlord shall first offer to lease the residential rental property in writing to the tenant displaced from that unit by the no-fault just cause termination if the tenant:","interaction":null,"confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
